d Johnson donated their Texas ranch house and about 600 acres to the National Park Service in 1972, but retained lifetime rights to use the property. After Lyndon Johnson died in 1973, Lady Bird continued living at the ranch until her death in 2007. The property, known as the “Texas White House”, was then prepared for public tours.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1972-lyndon-and-lady-bird-johnson-gave-the-texas-white-house-and-about-600-acres-to-the-us-but-kept-lifetime-rights-she-continued-living-there-until-2007/articleshow/133503507.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1972-lyndon-and-lady-bird-johnson-gave-the-texas-white-house-and-about-600-acres-to-the-us-but-kept-lifetime-rights-she-continued-living-there-until-2007/articleshow/133503507.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-25T16:23:03+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133505508,imgsize-149725.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[In 2022, Hunga Tonga sent water vapour into the stratosphere, altering ozone and radiation]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133503669.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[An eruption from an underwater volcano released a massive cloud of water vapor into the stratosphere, impacting global weather patterns for two years. This additional moisture led to an initial cooling effect on the stratosphere while also modifying circulation patterns. The presence of sulfate aerosols played a significant role in blocking sunlight, creating a stronger cooling influence.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/in-2022-the-hunga-tonga-eruption-sent-huge-amounts-of-water-vapour-into-the-stratosphere-causing-changes-in-ozone-levels-and-radiation-effects/articleshow/133503669.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/science/in-2022-the-hunga-tonga-eruption-sent-huge-amounts-of-water-vapour-into-the-stratosphere-causing-changes-in-ozone-levels-and-radiation-effects/articleshow/133503669.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-25T15:30:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI Science Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133503947,imgsize-1154822.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Colombian drug gangs recruiting children through social media to pilot explosive drones: Report]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133505407.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/colombian-drug-gangs-recruiting-children-through-social-media-to-pilot-explosive-drones-report/articleshow/133505407.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/rest-of-world/colombian-drug-gangs-recruiting-children-through-social-media-to-pilot-explosive-drones-report/articleshow/133505407.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-25T15:51:59+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">Prisha Patnaik</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133505581,imgsize-43000.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[In 2006, a gold mine became America’s deepest lab; scientists search for dark matter 4,850ft down]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133500780.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[A former gold mine in South Dakota has been transformed into a major underground science facility. The Homestake Mine once produced about 41 million ounces of gold and 9 million ounces of silver, but today its tunnels are used to study some of the Universe’s biggest mysteries. Scientists conduct experiments searching for dark matter, neutrinos and rare radioactive decay, while also researching microbes, geology and geothermal energy. Located 4,850 feet underground, the Sanford Underground Research Facility shows how a site once built for mining has found a new purpose in scientific discovery.]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-2006-south-dakota-began-turning-a-mine-that-produced-41-million-ounces-of-gold-into-americas-deepest-underground-lab-today-scientists-work-4850-feet-down-searching-for-dark-matter-and-neutrinos/articleshow/133500780.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-2006-south-dakota-began-turning-a-mine-that-produced-41-million-ounces-of-gold-into-americas-deepest-underground-lab-today-scientists-work-4850-feet-down-searching-for-dark-matter-and-neutrinos/articleshow/133500780.cms]]></guid><pubDate>2026-08-25T15:00:00+05:30</pubDate><dc:creator xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/">TOI World Desk</dc:creator><enclosure type="image/jpeg" url="https://static.toiimg.com/photo/msid-133502720,imgsize-117227.cms"/></item><item><title><![CDATA[Vermont to expand Snake Mountain WMA and secure 949 acres of timber rights]]></title><description>&lt;img border="0" hspace="10" align="left" style="margin-top:3px;margin-right:5px;" src="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/photo/133503694.cms" /&gt;<![CDATA[]]></description><link><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1959-vermont-bought-snake-mountain-land-but-the-seller-kept-timber-rights-67-years-later-the-state-plans-to-secure-949-acres-of-those-rights-and-add-256-acres/articleshow/133503694.cms]]></link><guid><![CDATA[https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/in-1959-vermont-bought-snake-mountain-land-but-the-seller-kept-timber-rights-67-years-later-the-state-plans-to-secure-949-acres-of-those-rights-and-add-256-acres/articleshow/1335036